{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012CYttwP4VbNhBRXZ9VmbUD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"The record kinds, typed. Each TypedDict here mirrors one JSON schema: its\n2\tkeys are that schema's `properties` and its required keys are that schema's\n3\t`required`, and tests/test_types_mirror_schemas.py asserts both, so the type\n4\tcannot drift from the contract. The schema stays the contract; this is its\n5\tmirror (issue #189).\n6\t\n7\tEvery key each schema lists is required, so no class here is total=False; a\n8\tkey the schema someday makes optional goes in a total=False base. A value the\n9\tschema declares as an object with fixed properties but this module does not\n10\tyet name is a dict[str, Any] (or a list of them), no tighter than the schema's\n11\town `additionalProperties` allows for the adapter-owned ones.\n12\t\"\"\"\n13\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n14\t\n15\tfrom typing import Any, Literal, NotRequired, TypedDict\n16\t\n17\t# The stage names a record file's <stage>-NN.json prefix can carry, one per\n18\t# schema under schema/records/.\n19\tStage = Literal[\"spec\", \"approval\", \"build\", \"verify\", \"counsel\"]\n20\t\n21\t# \"class\" is a Python keyword, so the record kinds that carry it are built\n22\t# with the functional syntax. signature and bound are R3's own additions,\n23\t# optional (a record older than record_version 8/7 carries neither).\n24\t_StopRequired = TypedDict(\"_StopRequired\", {\n25\t    \"class\": str,\n26\t    \"provenance\": str,\n27\t    \"detail\": str | None,\n28\t})\n29\t_StopOptional = TypedDict(\"_StopOptional\", {\n30\t    \"signature\": str | None,\n31\t    \"bound\": str | None,\n32\t}, total=False)\n33\t\n34\t\n35\tclass Stop(_StopRequired, _StopOptional):\n36\t    \"\"\"build-NN.json's and verify-NN.json's `stop`: the class of a stop and whose\n37\t    it is. The two schemas enumerate different classes; the shape is the same.\"\"\"\n38\t\n39\t\n40\tclass _SpecOp